Sval: an agent's window keeps the page it works in shown while hidden

Sval's agent works in its own window, which the user may minimise or
cover. The page the agent holds must stay visible and keep rendering
there, as in a window on screen, while every other tab goes hidden as
usual. Tabbrowser and its AsyncTabSwitcher decide which pages are active
from document.hidden alone, and the switcher keeps its own record of
each tab's layers; a page made active beside it breaks that record
(onLayersReady's assertion). No chrome API keeps a page active in a
hidden window: print preview, split view and Picture-in-Picture keep a
page loaded while hidden but cannot load one, as the switcher refuses
to load any tab while the window is hidden.

Tabbrowser.keepsPageShown names the page held shown, by the attribute
SvalAgentMarking puts on the agent's <browser>. The switcher treats a
window whose requested tab is held shown as one on screen, so it loads
and unloads that window's tabs through its own states. A hidden window
paints nothing, so the tab it switched from is unloaded without waiting
on a paint.
Tabbrowser.pagesShownChanged applies the window's visibility to its
pages again when the answer changes while the window is hidden.

